        // Set block.number to 15 (multiple of 15) and block.timestamp to 100
        vm.roll(15);
        vm.warp(100);
        // First call: send 10 ether from address(this)
        vm.deal(address(this), 10 ether);
        (bool success1, ) = address(c).call{value: 10 ether}("");
        require(success1, "First call failed");

        // Warp to a later timestamp, but keep block.number the same (still 15)
        vm.warp(200);
        // Second call: send 10 ether again
        vm.deal(address(this), 20 ether);
        (bool success2, ) = address(c).call{value: 10 ether}("");
        // On reference: success2 == false because pastBlockTime (101) > block.timestamp (200) condition fails? No, condition: require(block.timestamp > pastBlockTime) — now block.timestamp=200 > pastBlockTime=101, so it would succeed BUT block.number is still 15, so transfer happens. Actually, reference: pastBlockTime set to 101; second call: block.timestamp 200 > 101, succeeds, then transfer empties balance, and pastBlockTime becomes 201. Bug: pastBlockTime still 0; second call: block.timestamp 200 > 0, succeeds, then transfer empties balance. Both end with balance 0. Need different scenario.
        // Correct approach: Make block.number NOT a multiple of 15 so no transfer occurs; then the difference is pastBlockTime.
        // Reset: block.number 14 (not multiple of 15), first call at timestamp 100 sets pastBlockTime to 101 on reference; second call at timestamp 200 succeeds on reference (200 > 101), but on bug second call also succeeds (200 > 0). No divergence.
        // To diverge, use the condition block.timestamp > pastBlockTime in reverse: on bug, pastBlockTime stays 0, so a call at timestamp 1 ( > 0) succeeds, while on reference pastBlockTime=101, a call at timestamp 1 fails because 1 < 101.
        // New plan:
        // block.number = 1 (not multiple of 15)
        // warp to timestamp 100, first call: reference sets pastBlockTime=101, bug leaves 0
        // warp to timestamp 1 (before 100? No, warp must be >= previous? It can be any value, but timestamps are absolute, not relative. So after warp to 100, warp to 1 is possible? Yes, vm.warp can go backwards.
        // Then second call at timestamp 1: on reference, 1 > 101 is false => revert; on bug, 1 > 0 is true => succeeds.
        // So final balance: reference still has 10 ether (second call reverted), bug has 10 ether (second call succeeded, no transfer because block.number not multiple of 15).
        // Let's implement that.
        vm.roll(1);
        vm.warp(100);
        vm.deal(address(this), 10 ether);
        (bool ok1, ) = address(c).call{value: 10 ether}("");
